WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.793
Gentrification doesn't happen

00:00:01.793 --> 00:00:03.425
because people build new buildings,

00:00:03.425 --> 00:00:05.198
gentrification happens
because people don't.

00:00:05.198 --> 00:00:06.897
- [Presenter] This is a
video about housing in LA.

00:00:06.897 --> 00:00:09.183
but it relates to a lot of
cities in North America.

00:00:09.183 --> 00:00:11.468
It's about the cost of buying a house.

00:00:11.468 --> 00:00:13.731
I basically wanted to
know if me or my friends,

00:00:13.731 --> 00:00:15.406
anyone in the middle class,

00:00:15.406 --> 00:00:19.138
if we'd ever able to afford
a house in a city again.

00:00:19.138 --> 00:00:21.371
But this whole thing,
it actually started out

00:00:21.371 --> 00:00:23.648
with a much simpler idea for a video.

00:00:23.648 --> 00:00:26.537
LA Teachers react to LA Housing Prices.

00:00:26.537 --> 00:00:28.821
I mean, don't you think it's
common sense that teachers

00:00:28.821 --> 00:00:31.081
should be able to afford houses in

00:00:31.081 --> 00:00:33.825
the communities where they teach?

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:35.502
So I brought in all these teachers,

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:37.807
and I had them look at how
much house they could afford,

00:00:37.807 --> 00:00:40.215
using these basic online calculators.

00:00:40.215 --> 00:00:44.481
- OK, so it says I can
afford a $257,000 house

00:00:44.481 --> 00:00:47.314
with a mortgage payment of $1,301.

00:00:48.203 --> 00:00:49.845
- Wow. OK.

00:00:49.845 --> 00:00:53.680
Well, I could purchase
land but not a house.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.520
- So it's basically, like, in the armpit

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:56.353
of two freeways.

00:00:56.353 --> 00:00:57.186
- Yeah.

00:00:57.186 --> 00:00:58.216
- Yeah

00:00:58.216 --> 00:01:01.384
- [Presenter] So one bedroom, one bath.

00:01:01.384 --> 00:01:05.144
- Well, the bedroom is part of
the living room, looks like.

00:01:05.144 --> 00:01:06.445
- Ugh, ugh!

00:01:06.445 --> 00:01:08.200
- OK, let's say I could afford this.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.918
I'm also gonna get cancer.

00:01:09.918 --> 00:01:13.642
It's about 30 feet away from power lines.

00:01:13.642 --> 00:01:18.592
So that probably explains
the slightly lower price.

00:01:18.592 --> 00:01:20.741
- [Presenter] All that video
really illustrated was that

00:01:20.741 --> 00:01:22.583
houses do, indeed, cost too much.

00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:25.158
Teachers are, indeed, not paid enough

00:01:25.158 --> 00:01:26.784
and it was just kind of a bummer.

00:01:26.784 --> 00:01:27.617
- It's kind of depressing

00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:28.583
(laughs)

00:01:28.583 --> 00:01:31.251
to see what's out there
in our price range.

00:01:31.251 --> 00:01:33.171
- [Narrator] So the real video starts now.

00:01:33.171 --> 00:01:36.513
I talked to all these academics,
and they broke it down.

00:01:36.513 --> 00:01:38.587
It comes down to supply and demand.

00:01:38.587 --> 00:01:40.229
- The big challenge is when

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:43.778
there is a vibrant economic space,

00:01:43.778 --> 00:01:47.824
how do we accommodate new
migrants into that city?

00:01:47.824 --> 00:01:50.844
- I mean, the idea is housing
lasts for a really long time

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:52.752
A hundred years for a housing unit.

00:01:52.752 --> 00:01:56.202
And you build your housing
stock one year at a time,

00:01:56.202 --> 00:01:58.263
you can't build too many in any one year,

00:01:58.263 --> 00:02:00.188
and so if you have 10 years in a row

00:02:00.188 --> 00:02:01.661
where you don't build much,

00:02:01.661 --> 00:02:04.466
it creates a real hole in the
housing stock for the future.

00:02:04.466 --> 00:02:06.708
Because there's a housing shortage,

00:02:06.708 --> 00:02:10.263
it means that upper middle
class people move into

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:11.670
what are traditionally
working class neighborhoods

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.611
in order to afford a home

00:02:13.611 --> 00:02:16.739
and that ends up making it difficult for

00:02:16.739 --> 00:02:18.528
the traditional residents
of those neighborhoods

00:02:18.528 --> 00:02:21.382
to afford a place to live
in their own community.

00:02:21.382 --> 00:02:23.068
- So what we've ended up is the

00:02:23.068 --> 00:02:25.414
middle class housing
has been converted into

00:02:25.414 --> 00:02:28.457
rich people's housing and
the working man's housing

00:02:28.457 --> 00:02:30.985
has been turned into middle class housing

00:02:30.985 --> 00:02:34.202
and then the poor people's
housing has kind of been erased.

00:02:34.202 --> 00:02:35.710
And it's actually important to note that

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.168
the gentrification Professor
Myers is describing

00:02:38.168 --> 00:02:40.110
generally means it's communities of color

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.976
that are being displaced.

00:02:42.976 --> 00:02:44.526
To see what that looks like firsthand,

00:02:44.526 --> 00:02:46.729
I met up with this super
cool real estate agent.

00:02:46.729 --> 00:02:48.248
Her name's Rose Garcia,

00:02:48.248 --> 00:02:51.237
and she works in all these
developing neighborhoods.

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:52.339
She showed me around them,

00:02:52.339 --> 00:02:53.888
and we looked at these houses that,

00:02:53.888 --> 00:02:56.545
you know, five years ago
would have cost $100,000

00:02:56.545 --> 00:02:59.531
and are now going for
three or four times that.

00:02:59.531 --> 00:03:03.287
- This home, it's like less
than 1,000 square feet.

00:03:03.287 --> 00:03:05.291
I think it's like 1,100 square feet.

00:03:05.291 --> 00:03:07.379
It's for sale for $305,000.

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:09.781
And the problem is that
it's a total fix up,

00:03:09.781 --> 00:03:12.763
and not to mention, even
though it has a nice lot size,

00:03:12.763 --> 00:03:14.578
again, you're up against an alleyway.

00:03:14.578 --> 00:03:15.972
So this is the last kind of home

00:03:15.972 --> 00:03:18.016
that the standard, average, you know,

00:03:18.016 --> 00:03:20.177
city employee, teacher, anyone that makes

00:03:20.177 --> 00:03:23.033
around $65,000, $60,000 a year,

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:23.908
this is what you're getting.

00:03:23.908 --> 00:03:27.008
You're getting an
extreme super fixer upper

00:03:27.008 --> 00:03:29.830
in an area that's in the bud,

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:31.469
the budding of its gentrification.

00:03:31.469 --> 00:03:33.469
- [Presenter] Oh, and another
thing about this neighborhood.

00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:36.691
- There's wild chihuahua
packs that just run

00:03:36.691 --> 00:03:39.226
frivolously in the street
just, like, attacking people.

00:03:39.226 --> 00:03:40.086
It's crazy.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:41.234
- [Presenter] You don't
have that on the list ad.

00:03:41.234 --> 00:03:42.355
- No, they don't.

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:43.188
Well, welcome to east L.A.

00:03:43.188 --> 00:03:44.368
(laughs)

00:03:44.368 --> 00:03:45.799
I'm born and raised in Highland Park.

00:03:45.799 --> 00:03:49.024
Born in '74, lived in Highland
Park my whole entire life.

00:03:49.024 --> 00:03:50.612
I still live there now.

00:03:50.612 --> 00:03:52.693
And, you know, it's
amazing to me as an agent,

00:03:52.693 --> 00:03:54.698
I've been in this business 16 years,

00:03:54.698 --> 00:03:55.801
that people are paying, you know,

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:58.551
upwards near $700,000 for a house

00:03:59.396 --> 00:04:01.109
where I know people who've gotten shot at.

00:04:01.109 --> 00:04:02.212
I mean, it's crazy.

00:04:02.212 --> 00:04:03.357
That's part of what's happening in

00:04:03.357 --> 00:04:06.635
not only in L.A. but in the nation.

00:04:06.635 --> 00:04:08.202
Like I said, we own homes there.

00:04:08.202 --> 00:04:10.301
I'm happy that it's happening.

00:04:10.301 --> 00:04:13.409
It's just, it's happening really fast.

00:04:13.409 --> 00:04:15.131
And, you know, by the blink of an eye,

00:04:15.131 --> 00:04:16.353
I'm like, "What happened to my city?"

00:04:16.353 --> 00:04:17.601
It's just so different.

00:04:17.601 --> 00:04:19.354
- [Presenter] And so the
question I asked is like what

00:04:19.354 --> 00:04:22.285
can we do as a society or the city?

00:04:22.285 --> 00:04:25.968
Or what me, just as a dude
who cares about these things,

00:04:25.968 --> 00:04:27.472
how can we make it better?

00:04:27.472 --> 00:04:30.222
- Government really has to
allow more housing to be built.

00:04:30.222 --> 00:04:32.625
And then also to make sure
those who have the least

00:04:32.625 --> 00:04:34.626
amount of money can still afford housing.

00:04:34.626 --> 00:04:36.749
- It just takes too long
to get projects planned

00:04:36.749 --> 00:04:39.308
and get 'em approved and get 'em built.

00:04:39.308 --> 00:04:41.438
- In terms of general policy,

00:04:41.438 --> 00:04:43.788
to help create more housing in Los Angeles

00:04:43.788 --> 00:04:47.064
the number one thing I think
is like, is upzone the city.

00:04:47.064 --> 00:04:49.917
And by that we mean, like if
you look at any piece of land

00:04:49.917 --> 00:04:51.531
right in Los Angeles,

00:04:51.531 --> 00:04:53.598
there's gonna be rules
from the zoning code,

00:04:53.598 --> 00:04:55.430
how much units of
housing can you do there.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.864
And that's the restriction
that's really keeping us

00:04:57.864 --> 00:05:00.205
from creating enough
housing in Los Angeles.

00:05:00.205 --> 00:05:02.265
So we need to really
think, hey are we a city

00:05:02.265 --> 00:05:03.831
that actually wants a
lot of people to be here?

00:05:03.831 --> 00:05:04.955
That wants to be diverse?

00:05:04.955 --> 00:05:06.004
That wants to build transit?

00:05:06.004 --> 00:05:08.358
That wants to let people
have walkable communities?

00:05:08.358 --> 00:05:10.461
And if so, let's let there
be a little of density

00:05:10.461 --> 00:05:12.436
in a few other places other than just

00:05:12.436 --> 00:05:14.180
downtown and, like, Hollywood.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.584
Another obstacle to housing
is that every time you

00:05:16.584 --> 00:05:18.427
build anything in Los Angeles,

00:05:18.427 --> 00:05:21.121
you have to put in a certain
number of parking spaces,

00:05:21.121 --> 00:05:22.574
whether you need them or not.

00:05:22.574 --> 00:05:24.053
- In a lot of cases, there are two

00:05:24.053 --> 00:05:26.341
parking spots required per unit built.

00:05:26.341 --> 00:05:30.081
- And that kind of stuff drives
up the cost of construction

00:05:30.081 --> 00:05:33.594
and it makes it harder to meet
our needs for more housing.

00:05:33.594 --> 00:05:34.569
- [Presenter] So I met up with a guy.

00:05:34.569 --> 00:05:35.614
His name's Mott Smith,

00:05:35.614 --> 00:05:37.385
and he calls himself a social developer,

00:05:37.385 --> 00:05:38.654
and he really echoed that.

00:05:38.654 --> 00:05:39.923
He took this old bungalow court,

00:05:39.923 --> 00:05:41.273
and he turned it into town homes

00:05:41.273 --> 00:05:42.783
that were kinda like condos

00:05:42.783 --> 00:05:45.318
but were classified as
single-family homes,

00:05:45.318 --> 00:05:47.325
and for some kinda arcane reasons,

00:05:47.325 --> 00:05:49.463
were much cheaper to develop.

00:05:49.463 --> 00:05:51.986
- We're a developer doing
socially conscious projects

00:05:51.986 --> 00:05:53.900
in Los Angeles.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:57.809
We like to do things that
are regulatorily innovative.

00:05:57.809 --> 00:05:59.175
And so,

00:05:59.175 --> 00:06:03.373
Jane Blumenfeld, the planner
in the city of Los Angeles,

00:06:03.373 --> 00:06:06.358
said, "Why is it that
everything has to be a condo?"

00:06:06.358 --> 00:06:08.610
And so she worked for a couple of years

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:11.615
to craft something called
the Small Lot Ordinance,

00:06:11.615 --> 00:06:14.210
which actually lets you develop
townhouse-type buildings,

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:15.842
like that kind of density,

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:19.933
on small lots, so using
land much more efficiently.

00:06:19.933 --> 00:06:21.606
And simply by doing this,

00:06:21.606 --> 00:06:23.773
it's allowed developers like me

00:06:23.773 --> 00:06:25.039
to cut our costs to the point

00:06:25.039 --> 00:06:26.799
where we can afford to build

00:06:26.799 --> 00:06:30.242
at a price that more
people can afford to buy.

00:06:30.242 --> 00:06:32.715
It doesn't solve the affordability problem

00:06:32.715 --> 00:06:33.982
by any stretch of the imagination,

00:06:33.982 --> 00:06:35.661
but when we did this project,

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:37.254
this was still the lowest priced

00:06:37.254 --> 00:06:40.923
new-quality single-family
housing in the area.

00:06:40.923 --> 00:06:42.397
The future's not finding a way

00:06:42.397 --> 00:06:46.344
to do cul-de-sacs and
5,000 square foot houses

00:06:46.344 --> 00:06:49.046
on 10,000 square foot lots and
somehow make that affordable

00:06:49.046 --> 00:06:50.032
to the world.

00:06:50.032 --> 00:06:51.626
I don't think that's a winning strategy.

00:06:51.626 --> 00:06:53.718
I think the future is
coming up with new ways

00:06:53.718 --> 00:06:54.784
of building housing,

00:06:54.784 --> 00:06:57.063
new ways of being in the city,

00:06:57.063 --> 00:07:00.613
and using all of our
resources more efficiently.

00:07:00.613 --> 00:07:01.927
- [Presenter] And that's just one example

00:07:01.927 --> 00:07:04.427
of a rule changed to city laws

00:07:04.427 --> 00:07:07.568
that let the developer
build more for cheaper.

00:07:07.568 --> 00:07:09.484
So we definitely need
to encourage the city

00:07:09.484 --> 00:07:10.964
to make it easier to build.

00:07:10.964 --> 00:07:11.905
But overall,

00:07:11.905 --> 00:07:15.076
we as a generation just need
to pay a lot more attention

00:07:15.076 --> 00:07:18.329
to the health of the
housing stock in our cities.

00:07:18.329 --> 00:07:20.484
Most people are just kinda
in it for themselves.

00:07:20.484 --> 00:07:23.185
As long as they can afford to
buy a house or rent a house,

00:07:23.185 --> 00:07:27.418
the overall housing stock
doesn't matter to them.

00:07:27.418 --> 00:07:28.663
But the truth is,

00:07:28.663 --> 00:07:29.860
If you actually wanna live in a city

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:32.946
that is a cool, diverse,
interesting place,

00:07:32.946 --> 00:07:34.612
this is, like, one of the main things

00:07:34.612 --> 00:07:36.418
that you should pay attention to

00:07:36.418 --> 00:07:38.995
in, like, the politics of your city.

00:07:38.995 --> 00:07:41.919
And that's why I made this video.

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:45.086
(Jazz trumpet music)

